Asking for a window number must not build a window device.

-[NSWindow cider_platformWindow] is LAZY: it creates the platform window, and therefore a
compositor surface, on the first ask. -[NSWindow windowNumber] asked it unconditionally, so reading
an INTEGER off a window that is never shown created a Wayland surface for it.

Measured with CIDER_TRACE_APP on a run of iTerm2 Command comma, the twelve asks for a platform
window break down as

    7  -[NSCachedImageRep initWithSize:depth:separate:alpha:]
    2  -[NSWindow windowNumber]
    2  -[NSWindow setFrame:display:animate:]
    1  -[NSWindow orderWindow:relativeTo:]

The seven image cache asks are fine: NSCachedImageRep backs an NSImage with a real NSWindow
carrying NSAppKitPrivateWindow, 0x8000000, and those get backing without a compositor surface,
which is the handling they are supposed to get. windowNumber is the one that should never have
cost anything.

AppKit answers 0 for a window with no window device, which is exactly what a deferred or never
shown window is, so that is what this answers. Measured: the compositor sees FOUR xdg_surfaces in
that run instead of five.

THIS DOES NOT FIX THE BLANK SCREEN and it is not claimed to. iTerm2 Preferences still blanks the
display once the nib decode completes, 2 of 2 runs with the guard in. What the guard does is remove
one spurious surface and stop every future never shown window from paying for an integer.

WHAT THE SAME SESSION LEARNED ABOUT THE BLANKING, recorded in docs/iterm2-preferences-gap.md: the
compositor tree at the black moment has an EMPTY workspace. There is an output HEADLESS-1 at
1256x684 and a workspace 1 at 1256x684 and NOT ONE CONTAINER on it, while our backend still reports
the terminal mapped and logs no unmap and no destroy. The control, the same build with no
keystroke, has con vis=True rect=1256x684+0+0. So the terminal view is being removed from the
compositor side. on_popup_done is NOT the path: it prints popup=dismissed and that line never
appears in a black run.

